WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.331
A probability distribution specifies how probability is allocated across possible values

00:00:05.331 --> 00:00:10.261
In a discrete distribution, probabilities are nonnegative and add to one

00:00:10.261 --> 00:00:17.433
These three bars assign one fifth, three tenths, and one half to zero, one, and two.

00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:26.011
A binomial model counts successes across a fixed number of independent trials with the same success probability

00:00:26.011 --> 00:00:35.000
For three fair tosses, exactly one head can occur in three positions, each with probability one eighth, giving three eighths altogether.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.153
A continuous distribution uses density, whose area gives probability

00:00:40.153 --> 00:00:45.289
Uniform density one half from zero to two has total area one

00:00:45.289 --> 00:00:52.567
An interval of length one inside this range has probability one half, regardless of where it lies.

00:00:53.867 --> 00:00:57.946
Density is not a point probability and may exceed one

00:00:57.946 --> 00:01:04.065
Uniform density four on an interval of length one quarter still has total probability one

00:01:04.065 --> 00:01:11.000
Any single exact point has probability zero in this continuous model because its interval width is zero.
